        "text": "Over the past two years of building production‑grade recommendation and fraud models, I’ve run into three “edge‑case” evaluation gaps that are easy to miss but cheap to expose with the right tests. 1. Sparse‑token explosion in multilingual text – In a language‑agnostic sentiment classifier (BERT‑base‑uncased, v2.2), a sudden rollout of a new market in West Africa added a high‑frequency Swahili token set that the tokenizer split into over 150 sub‑tokens per sentence. Offline validation used English‑only data, so the average token count was 32. In production the inference graph grew from 32 × 768 to 150 × 768, inflating GPU memory usage by 4.7× and causing out‑of‑memory errors on the 8 GB T4 containers. The first symptom was a spike in “InternalServerError” logs, not a dip in accuracy. Adding a pre‑deployment token‑length guard (maxtokens=80) caught the issue before the canary. 2. Feature‑store version drift – Our feature pipeline relied on AWS SageMaker Feature Store v2.3. In March 2026 a security patch rolled out v2.4 with a different default serialization for categorical embeddings (int64 → int32). The downstream PyTorch inference code (torch 2.3) expected int64 and silently truncated the high‑order bits, corrupting 0.3 % of embedding lookups. The model’s AUC dropped from 0.991 to 0.987 on the hidden validation set, but the offline suite never exercised the new store version. A shadow test that forces the feature store client to use the latest API version revealed the mismatch. 3. Time‑zone‑aware timestamp rounding – A fraud model consumed a transactionts column that was stored in UTC but displayed in the UI as the user’s local time. A recent UI refactor switched from millisecond epoch to ISO‑8601 strings with millisecond precision. The feature extractor performed integer division (ts // 1000) assuming seconds, which introduced a +0.001 s drift per request. Over a million daily transactions this added up to ≈1 GB of extra feature storage and caused